# Recheck / close checks for every movement (coordinator standard, 2026-10-04)

Every movement closes only after a recheck (Sol by default) that has read the FULL current movement and covers:

1. **Brief items.** Each one RESOLVED, PARTIAL or NOT, with quoted evidence (diff vs `pre-repair/`).
2. **Continuity.** Against BOOK_MAP, STATE_LEDGER rulings, the previous movement and later-book canon. Covers calendar, counts, knowledge boundaries, protected lines (exact; first where mapped) and reserved truths.
3. **Formula.** `formula_metrics.py` reproduced. Overlap 0 unprotected. Gates 0. `sweep_probe.sh` totals.
4. **Reader clarity.** Speaker attribution, referents, the 13-year-old lens, and the Reader Standard.
5. **Listening proof.** For every changed region and every chapter of the movement, check:
   - balanced quotes and italics;
   - `---` spacing;
   - numerals and abbreviations the narrator would misvoice;
   - homographs and ear collisions;
   - broken joins.
6. **Output.** A verdict (CLOSE / CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES / SECOND REPAIR), then exact line fixes in the `N. \`manuscript/chapter-NN.md\` / old: / new:` format, each verified to match once.

On CLOSE, the coordinator:
- applies the fixes;
- appends the STATE_LEDGER "After Movement N" block and its CLOSED note;
- records AUTHORSHIP (actual model) and the RUN-LEDGER line;
- commits and pushes;
- publishes the movement's chapters to the PWA edition (text only; bump totalChapters).
# REPAIR BRIEF r1 — Book 8, Movement 5 "No Authority" (ch27–33)

Same author, same method (NO-SOURCE: do not open `books/` or `v3-runs/`). You may read this edition's own closed text and the plan. The four Sol reads are in this folder (`plan-audit.md`, `source-tracking.md`, `review-editorial.md`, `review-cold.md`); read them before you touch a line. The baseline is `pre-repair-r1/` (your draft plus the eight coordinator-applied fixes in `plan-audit-apply.md`, and the notice set in italics). Write `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md` when done and refresh AUTHOR-REPORT's "New canon minted" and "State at movement end".

**Verdict on the draft:** the audit ratified the architecture, every piece of staging and all nineteen flags; no minted item is amended or cut. Nothing is to be re-staged. The yard door, the dray, the night walk, the decipherment and the alley stand. This is a bounded repair: one scene's worth of re-joining in chapter 27 where two protected lines turned out to be Cleon's, joins round six other restored units, one tracked passage, and a light repetition pass. Do not grow the movement (about 36,070 against ~35,000 is accepted; item C3 should take a little out).

## A. KEPT (ruled; not to change)

All nineteen flags as you staged them: the eleventh's manner of coming and its prompt (*This far?*); the antecedent of "it" in Vastin's line; Seln's case "shut" (never "unopened"); Seln on the hatch's bench and the cost of his truth; the clerk's difficulty and one question; Cleon's week from the 287th to the 293rd; the second week of the loft and the purse at 134; the decipherment's figures; Seln's finding about the faction; the rail, the inner stair, the galleries, the bell. Deployment thirty-two; eleven fragments.

**The eleventh's lines (ch33):** your frame stands — Cael puts the lines into the plain words in which he will have to write them. The audit wanted the unit printed as system text under a FRAGMENT ACQUIRED heading; that is declined (the unit is Cael's own cataloguing, names Vastin and says "Eleventh"; the twelfth, in the same form, says "me"). The coordinator has taken the unit out of its code fence and set it in italics, since a fenced block in this edition is the system's own text. Check the paragraph before and after it still reads true ("The lines were where they had always been…").

## B. Already applied by the coordinator (keep the protected words exactly; re-join in your voice)

1. **ch27, the Hall's table — TWO PROTECTED LINES ARE CLEON'S, NOT KARIS'S.** Now on the page: `Cleon said, "Being believed — " He stopped. "I had thought it would be a relief. It's not. It's the end of being able to be wrong."` and, a little later, `Cleon opened his eyes. "Give me a week."` This changes who the scene's turn belongs to. It is the man in the chair who has been believed: he has had a board for ten years and a hand that nobody else knew, and a stranger has walked in with three floors that match it. He says so, unasked, from his chair, with his eyes shut, in the hearing of all of them; and it is he who asks for the week. Re-compose from "Karis did not go away from the third table" to the scene's end so that this is true: Cael's "Then what is it?" may still be put to Karis and go unanswered by her (she can be shaking and say nothing, or say only what she can show) while the answer comes from the desk; Brom still prices a week; **Karis then asks the five for the same week** in her own words (the protected log in ch28 says "Karis says give her a week", so she must have said it — not in the protected words). Later lines that leaned on the old assignment must follow: ch27's "It had not come into his mind to be afraid of the other" (whose fear is it now?); ch30 where Cleon says "A week… Mine. From today." (he is now re-setting his own earlier week to run from the 287th, with everything else put aside: make that plain in a clause); ch31's opening, where Karis thinks about having been believed (it may stand as her side of the same thing, but must not restate his sentence).
2. **ch28, the hatch — Seln's line, whole:** `"He's on the count. Four hands, same as yesterday, entered by the practitioner and answered for by her, and the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was. The researcher's at your counter and she'll want to be told we're through," Seln said.` Check the paragraphs after it: your account of what he did NOT say, and "every word… would have stood under two hands", must match these words (he says "we"; he names "the practitioner" and "the researcher" — Cleon's card, and Karis at the ledge). Do not paraphrase the line anywhere.
3. **ch29:** `Cleon said, "They're orders."` and `Cleon said, "Same make."` — your staging was right. Vary the two attributions if they sit badly; the words are fixed. Karis's answer to "Same make." is still unheard by Cael, which is fine.
4. **ch30, Cleon's close, whole.** The four spoken pieces are fixed. Between them the coordinator has put three plain joins ("His eyes went down the gallery to the plank." / "He rubbed his eyes." / "He stopped.") — replace them with your own; he is standing at his desk with two fingers lately on the bundle's tape and has just looked straight at her for the first time. He uses her name: your paragraph after it already says so.

## C. For you to recompose

1. **One tracked passage (source-tracking.md): ch27:101–107**, the wait while Cleon reads to the foot of his page. The chain is: the group follows his progress down the page → he reaches the end → marks his place with a slip → turns the leaf over → turns round. Break the order, do not reword. (The plan needs only that he is already reading and finishes his page before he attends to them. He might speak with his back still turned; the leaf might be marked later.)
2. **The cipher proof (ch31:3–190), both reviews.** A listener may follow Karis's principle and lose which repetition proves which letter. At two natural turns, give a physical anchor (her pencil on the two places; the boy's lamp moved from one to the other); do not add a proof step and do not advance the leaf.
3. **The rule said too often (cold read item 2), ch27–31.** "Look at how it stands; do not guess; wait for a second independent match" is demonstrated well and then restated several times in narration and dialogue. Keep every discovery and every test; where a paragraph only restates the method, compress or cut it. A few short paragraphs in all.
4. **First uses for a reader arriving here (cold read item 3):** at the earliest consequential use in this movement of Anchor's "*There?*", "the read", and a bracketed status word, one anchoring phrase each. No recap.
5. Do NOT undertake the editorial's wider register pass (its item 2); the formula ranges pass.

## D. Standing constraints (unchanged)

Season-blind; feet, yards, miles, paces, strides, cords; no year count since the Kindling in narration; nineteen; no possessive before "Path" in narration; no new proper names; Vastin without an age; Cael never on the square or the steps and never looking at the crown; Shadow sealed; Seln's case shut and its contents unnamed; Cleon's Path shown from outside only; the leaf unread; the eleventh neither deployed nor told; consent explicit; every protected unit whole and once. Every chapter inside the three working ranges; gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (`bash editions/monroe-1.3/tools/ed.sh overlap book-08-before-the-paths 5`).
# REPAIR REPORT r1 — Book 8, Movement 5 "No Authority" (ch27–33)

Same author (`claude-opus-5-5`, O'Connor 1.3 seat), same session, same method: NO-SOURCE. No file under `books/` or `v3-runs/` was opened; no git command was run. Read before touching a line: `REPAIR-BRIEF.md`, `plan-audit-apply.md`, and the four Sol reads (`plan-audit.md`, `source-tracking.md`, `review-editorial.md`, `review-cold.md`). Baseline: `pre-repair-r1/` (the draft plus the coordinator's eight fixes, the notice in italics), confirmed identical to the manuscript on disk before I began. Chapters 32 and 33 are untouched in this round.

The plan audit quotes three of the source's stage directions in its fix 8, and the source's "He stopped." inside the "Being believed" unit. None of the three stage directions is in the manuscript; "He stopped." stands exactly as the coordinator restored it, inside the unit.

## What was done

### B1 — chapter 27, the Hall's table: the turn is Cleon's (ch27:203–273)

Re-composed from "Karis did not go away from the third table" to the scene's end.

- Cael still puts "Then what is it?" to Karis. She is shaking and **does not answer him**.
- **The desk answers** (ch27:225–227). The desk is not six strides off, nothing can be said low enough where the air does not move, and Cleon has not opened his eyes. The protected unit stands whole, with his name in the sentence before it and no tag of mine inside it: `"Being believed — " He stopped. "I had thought it would be a relief. It's not. It's the end of being able to be wrong."` He says it to the dark behind his own lids and gives no sign of knowing that five people heard.
- Karis turns round and looks at him "as a person looks who has heard her own trouble told in a stranger's voice". That is all of her side on the day; she does not say his sentence or anything like it.
- **Whose fear it is now:** "Cael had no answer for either of them… It had not come into his mind that the man might have something of his own to lose by nodding."
- Brom asks who has believed him, since he has told nobody anything. Karis answers with what she can show: he has had a thing on that shelf, in sacking, with a hand in it that no other person knew; her fifty-two sheets agree with it; she does not suppose that anything has agreed with him before. (She does not know yet how long he has had it; the ten years are his own word on the 286th.)
- Lira asks how long it will take him; Karis says that it is his to say. **At the desk the red eyes come open, and Cleon, looking at three tables, says `"Give me a week."`** (ch27:249). They shut again.
- Brom prices it (unchanged). Vastin's line and the tags are unchanged.
- **Karis asks the five for the same week in her own words** (ch27:263): "It is not mine to promise him… But I am asking you for it. I should like his week." Cael: "She's asked for a week, and it's hers." The protected log's "Karis says give her a week" is therefore true, and the protected three words are Cleon's alone, once.
- Karis's three kinds of work close the scene as before, a sentence shorter.

**Carried into chapter 30 (ch30:257).** Cleon now re-sets his own earlier week: "I asked you for one, the first day, and I've spent the half of it on my own page; I've read that every morning before I'd so much as look at yours. That's put by. A week. Mine. From today, and nothing else on the desk." Karis's "A week from today is the two hundred and ninety-third" follows as before.

**Carried into chapter 31 (ch31:9–13).** The opening no longer explains what being believed costs. It now says that he "had said another thing, three days before, from his chair, with his eyes shut, and had said it of himself", that she had not answered and had known it for her own as well, and it keeps only the light pencil of a person whom nobody attends to and the pencil that is not light tonight. The sentence about drawing a line wrong and rubbing it out is cut. "The place where he had stopped to rub his eyes" is now "the place where his fingers had gone back to the tape", to agree with the close's new joins.

### B2 — chapter 28, the hatch (ch28:71–99)

Seln's restored line stands whole, with the coordinator's "Seln said." The sentence before it is re-joined ("It was the clerk that he looked at, and not the old man; and he used the mild voice…"). My account of what he did and did not say now matches his words without paraphrasing them: "He had told a clerk nothing but what lay already in the clerk's own book, or stood at the clerk's own ledge; and he had said *we*, which lay in no book at all." The three things he did not say, "would have stood under two hands", and "joined it with his own mouth to four porters and a card" are unchanged and agree with the line as restored. The clerk's *The same* now follows naturally on a line that has "same as yesterday" in it; I did not have him repeat it.

### B3 — chapter 29, the two attributions

`Cleon said, "They're orders."` stands as the coordinator set it (ch29:219). The second is varied: "…put both hands in the small of his back. Cleon looked from the plank to the three tables, and at the plank again." / `"Same make."` (ch29:275–277). The words are fixed; Karis's answer is still unheard.

### B4 — chapter 30, Cleon's close (ch30:273)

The four spoken pieces are untouched. The coordinator's three joins are replaced with mine, from where he stands (at his desk, turned round to her, the shelf behind him):
1. "His chin went toward the three tables, and his eyes did not leave her."
2. "The two fingers found the brown tape again, behind him, without his looking for it."
3. "He let the hand fall from the shelf."

The paragraph after it (nobody spoke; he had used her name) is unchanged.

### C1 — the tracked passage, chapter 27 (now ch27:95–123)

The order is broken; nothing was merely reworded. He is still already reading and still finishes his page before he attends to them, which is all the plan needs. New order: they stop at the edge of his light → he raises a hand and reads on → **he speaks to the leaf, with his back turned and his finger on its line**, asks which place is which and assigns the three tables → "Get your loads off. I'll come when I've done here." → the loads come off; Karis waits; Cael watches Vastin be kept standing → **when Cael looks at the desk again the chair is empty**: the man is at the first bare table, and the leaf lies face upward with nothing across it to keep the place; "he had come to the foot of it and walked away, and not one of five people had seen him do either." → the lamp's rule. Gone: Lira counting his lines to the foot, the finger reaching the page's foot, the slip, the leaf turned face down, the turning round.

### C2 — two physical anchors in the cipher proof (ch31:83, ch31:89)

No proof step added; the leaf is no nearer.
1. At the first repetition: "She set the pencil's point under the one and the tip of her little finger under the other, and had the pair of them between the two ends of her own hand: third and last, as the old clerk's *twelve* had them."
2. At the cross-check between two words: "She kept her pencil on it, and said *up*; and the boy carried the light up the plank to the first row, where the fifth of the six stood out of the dark, and it was a fork. He brought the flame down again without being told."

### C3 — the rule said too often (ch27–31)

Every discovery and test is kept. Cut or compressed where a paragraph only restated the method:
- ch27: the looking at the Ring's eleven is one sentence shorter (the eyes going "from the foot" is shown once); Karis's "I have one man who can see how a thing stands… I shall not be hurried over it" is gone with the old scene; the three kinds of work lose a sentence.
- ch28: "The eyes began where the weight came down, and they asked the pencil for nothing that it had not offered" is cut (Cael at the lamp); "He had asked how a thing was made, and been told, and what he had made of the telling went nowhere" is cut.
- ch29: "It did not give her the rest. She said so, twice, to nobody" is cut; Karis's "I shall want every row…" is one sentence.
- ch30: Seln's doorway sighting no longer restates the rule of a thing seen once ("He put it where he put a date read once by lamplight… before anything was done" is cut; he says it himself in ch31).
- ch31: "She had a sentence for occasions of this kind…" and the paragraph after it are one sentence; "She would not trust her memory…" is one clause; the five ways to fail are one sentence, and "She was not happy… the old plain pleasure of having found a way to be proved wrong" is cut; "glad of it as a mason is glad of a second plumb" is cut; the bundle and the page lose their restatements ("She would only read into it what she had been told to expect"; "If she showed him that word, he would see it in everything…").

### C4 — three first uses anchored

- **Anchor's *There?*** (ch27:35): "Anchor was the eighth of the ten things that he carried, a piece of the craft by which the Compact holds a person where he stands; it had asked his leave to bar every sill and gate since Ostrand…"
- **A bracketed status word** (ch27:183, at `[UNBOUND]`): "It was a registrar's word, which the Compact had once written in square brackets against whatever its plates could not class, and had long since struck out of its books; and it was the oldest thing that she knew how to date by."
- **"The read"** (ch29:33): "It was the third of the ten that he carried, a piece of Brom's own Iron come to him at second hand…"

### C5 — not done, as instructed

No register pass.

### A — the eleventh's lines (ch33:153–157)

Checked. The frame sentence ("He took them as he had taken the last… putting each into the plain words in which he would have to write it"), the italic unit and "Eleven." read true together, and the paragraphs after (what is new in it; "the lines had found a name for that") still follow. Nothing changed.

