books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:45:"It's that, longer." He put his hand down flat on the table beside the third set, not on it. "I'll tell you the thing I tell nobody, because you've earned it by walking in, and because you'll need to know it before you sit down in this chair, which you're going to. Being right for thirty years alone is a thing a man can carry. Being believed — " He stopped. "I had thought it would be a relief. It's not. It's the end of being able to be wrong. I've been wrong, safely, in this room, for thirty years. Nobody read the work, so the work could be anything. Now it's going to be read, and it's going to be *this*" — the hand a finger's width above the sheets — "and if I've been right, then what I've been right about is not a thing a man is glad to be right about." He looked at her for a moment longer. "Give me a week. Give me a week with these and the text and I'll tell you what you've got. And then you'll not sleep either, and I'm sorry for it, and I'm not going to stop."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:135:"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." He tapped the board. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." He sat back. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." A beat. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."

