editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-54.md:95:He lay down carefully on his left side, and put out the lamp, and lay a long time in the dark listening to the river and to the small click in his ribs, before he slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:175:"You're reading me bored." He nodded to the baker, who had been listening to all of it with great interest, and turned to go on down the hill. Then he turned back. "Thank you for the bread," he said to Lira, very seriously, and went.
