# Chapter 10 — The Deepstacks

The dark did not give the lamp back, and then it did, a little at a time, in the order the lamp asked for it.

Rock first. The quarry's own rock, cut in the days before anyone cut rock square, in galleries that went off from the landing in three directions at a height a man could walk in and a width two men could pass in, and the cut-marks still on every face of it, the marks of tools nobody made any more, running as the quarrymen had run them and not as a mason would. Then the shelving, which was not shelving as the Compact shelved a thing — no cases, no doors, no labels — but timber pinned into the rock floor to roof along both walls of every gallery, grey with age and dry as bone, and on the timber, the records.

He catalogued them by their shapes because he could not catalogue them by anything else. Rolls, the size of a man's forearm, tied, with the ties gone to dust and the rolls staying rolled by habit. Boards — flat pieces of wood the length of a hand with a face to them, a hundred to a shelf, standing on their edges like slates. Bound leaves, thick, sewn along one side with something that had been cord, in covers of a leather that had been a colour once. Things that were none of those: a sheet of hammered metal with marks on it, standing against the rock; a stone, squared, with a face cut into it; a bundle of sticks tied in a pattern that was, he understood after a while, the pattern itself, and not a bundle. Centuries of a country's paper, if it was paper, in scripts that were not the Compact's and had not been anyone's for longer than the Compact had had a name, kept because a decision had never been made about them, in a hole, in the dark, under a building whose whole purpose was to decide.

And the smell. He had no catalogue for it. It was the smell of a thing that has been left alone for so long that the leaving-alone has become the thing, and he stood on the landing with a box on his shoulder and breathed it and understood that he had never once, in nineteen years, stood in a place where nothing had been decided, and that this was what it smelled like.

The lamp went on into the middle gallery, and there was a light at the end of it that had gone ahead of them.

---

Cleon was already reading.

That was the truest thing about the man, and the first: they came down the middle gallery with the lamp and the boxes, five people and the sound of five people in a place that had not heard five people in longer than the walls had stood, and at the gallery's end, where the rock opened into a chamber that the quarrymen had left when they hit a seam they did not want, there was a table, and a lamp on it that was always lit, and a man at the table with a bound thing open in front of him and his finger on a line, reading, who did not look up.

He had gone ahead down the middle gallery with his own lamp while the hands got the boxes off the landing, and had been at the table long enough to find his line before they reached it. He had a chair with a cushion on it that had been a cushion for thirty years, and a second lamp on a bracket in the rock above the table that a hand had put there to throw light where a reader wanted it, and behind the table, along the chamber's back wall, shelving that was not the archive's — newer timber, squared, with a man's own order on it: bound leaves in one hand, a hand Cael knew from a ledger in a coat, hundreds of them, thirty years of a man writing down what he had read in a room nobody else read in, and the room's own things pulled from the galleries and stood on the new shelves where he could reach them, in an order that was his and would die with him.

He read to the end of the line. Then to the end of the page. Then he put a scrap of leather in it and closed the thing and put both hands flat on it, which was, Cael saw, exactly what Karis did, and looked up, and looked at Karis, and at nothing else in the chamber.

"Put them there," he said, and moved the bound thing off the table's middle.

Karis put them there.

She did it as she had put a ring on a table and a wall on a floor and a marshal's six years on a slab: without saying she was going to, and all at once, and in the order that was in her head. The oilcloth opened. Fifty-two sheets, in three sets, laid out along the table's length in the order of the floors — the Ring at the left end, eleven; the Stair in the middle, twenty-nine; the Hall at the right, twelve — with the lamp moved once to put the light where the sets met, and the charcoal on the sheets grey against the paper as it had been grey on a floor in the dark, and she did not say a word while she laid them, and neither did he, and the four hands stood with the boxes against the chamber's wall and watched a man look at fifty-two sheets that he had, Cael thought, been looking for in some part of himself since before any of the four had been born.

He did not touch them. He looked. He looked at the first set for as long as it takes a man to read a page he already knows, and then at the second, longer, and then at the third, and his hand came up off the table and went toward the third set and stopped, a hand's width off, and stayed there, and Cael saw it stay and had seen a hand do that before, on a table, near a page.

"Three floors," Cleon said.

"Three floors. Two hundred metres each, to the line. A fourth we didn't go in."

"A fourth." His hand had not moved. "And the word."

Karis said it. "Older than [UNBOUND]," she said, as she had said it at a table in the autumn, once, as a stratum's limit and not as a meaning — the old word, the retired one, the one from the confiscated record that she had carried across two seasons and a Line and had put in a sentence exactly once — and Cleon closed his eyes.

Not for long. For the length of a man deciding whether a thing he has hoped for and dreaded in equal measure for thirty years is the one or the other, and finding that it is both, and that both is not what the young man who started the work had imagined it would be. He opened them. He looked at the third set, at the Hall's twelve sheets, at a mark on the fourth of them that Cael could not have told from any other mark, and said, quietly, to the sheet and not to Karis:

"I know that hand." He did not say from where. "You've brought it in off the ground. Do you know what that is, to a man who's been sitting in a hole for thirty years being tolerated?"

"I know what a marsh with a kit's ledger in it was to me," Karis said. "I'd guess it's that, longer."

"It's that, longer." He put his hand down flat on the table beside the third set, not on it. "I'll tell you the thing I tell nobody, because you've earned it by walking in, and because you'll need to know it before you sit down in this chair, which you're going to. Being right for thirty years alone is a thing a man can carry. Being believed — " He stopped. "I had thought it would be a relief. It's not. It's the end of being able to be wrong. I've been wrong, safely, in this room, for thirty years. Nobody read the work, so the work could be anything. Now it's going to be read, and it's going to be *this*" — the hand a finger's width above the sheets — "and if I've been right, then what I've been right about is not a thing a man is glad to be right about." He looked at her for a moment longer. "Give me a week. Give me a week with these and the text and I'll tell you what you've got. And then you'll not sleep either, and I'm sorry for it, and I'm not going to stop."

"Noted," Karis said, and sat down, in the second chair, which had not had a cushion on it, and which Cleon had brought down that morning.

---

He set the box down.

He catalogued that, because it was the whole of his function in the room and a man should know his function to the hair. The middle box, against the chamber's wall, beside the first and the third and the kit's case, lid up, so that Karis could reach the sheets in it — the fair copies, the ledger, the map, the two brass edges — without asking, and then nothing. Nothing to carry. Nothing to count. Nothing to be, in a chamber under a city, but a body that had carried a box down a stair and would carry it up again at the bell, standing against a rock wall with three other bodies while two people who could read the room began to.

He had not been nothing inside walls before. He had been a case, a hearing, a designation, a boy with a number on a sheet; he had been the thing every room he stood in was about. He stood against the rock and was not the thing this room was about, and it was, he found — the finding arrived slowly, over the first hour, as the lamp's light had arrived — the first place inside a wall he had ever stood where nobody could file him. Not would not. *Could* not. The stone was older than the Compact. The Compact's reach was a thing that went down through its own foundation and stopped where the rock changed, because the rock had never been told what the Compact was and the Compact had never once sent a man down the stair to tell it. Everything in this chamber was older than the machine that filed. The paper was. The shelves were. The dark was. He was standing in the one hole in the whole of Valdris that the classification had never been lowered into, and he was standing in it as a number, as labour, as a hand, and the number had stopped at the door because the door was older than numbers.

He wrote that down, later, in the log, in the plain hand, at the length it deserved, which was one line, because a thing that is true of a wall does not get truer for being written longer. At the wall he only stood in it.

Brom, beside him, had set his box down and had sat on it, with the shoulder still and forward and the foot out, and was watching Karis work with the face he kept for somebody else pricing a wall: respect, and no intention of touching it. Lira was on her feet, with her two hands at her sides, and was not watching Karis; she was watching the galleries going off into the dark in three directions, the way she watched a floor for the place where a thing would come from, because a hole was a hole. And Vastin had set the kit's case down and stood beside it on the knee, with the stick, and was looking at the shelving behind Cleon's chair — the thirty years of one man's hand — with an expression Cael had not seen on him and had, after a while, a name for: it was the look of a man who has spent a lifetime in offices reading what other men filed, and has just understood that this man had filed the whole of his life in a hole where no one would ever read it, and had done it anyway, and had been right.

At the table, Cleon had drawn the Hall's fourth sheet toward him and was reading it alone, and his finger was on a mark on the sheet, and he was not reading what the mark said. Cael could not have said how he knew that. He knew it the way he knew a thing about a floor with the Iron read on it: the man's attention was not on the mark's meaning; it was on the mark's *bones* — on which of the lines that made it were holding the others up — and he was reading the bones, one mark after another, and going on, and not saying anything, because a man who has been reading alone for thirty years does not say the thing until the thing is checked.

The bell came down the stair at the end of the day, faint, a city's bell four tiers up, and they went up to it with the boxes, and left Cleon at his table with the lamp that was always lit, reading.

---

The second morning was where the door nearly closed.

He catalogued it because it was the morning's cost and the cost was not his, and a man who keeps a ledger keeps other people's costs in it too.

The provision was worked once and stood for the week; that was the form. But the yard door was a door, with a keeper, and a keeper had a book, and the book wanted the count each morning that the count went through it — *four*, again, entered again, by the same careful young man with the same pen, who had looked at four faces yesterday for as long as he looked at four boxes and had written the number. He was there again on the two hundred and eighty-fifth morning, in the yard door, with the pen, and the four of them were in the lane with the boxes, and Karis was inside at the counter with Cleon, and Seln, who walked the party to the door each morning and did not go through it, was at the lane's mouth with his hands in his coat.

The clerk counted. One, two, three — and stopped on the fourth, which was Vastin, and Cael saw why: the man had the kit's case on his shoulder and the stick in his other hand and the knee had been asked to stand in a lane for a quarter of an hour on a stone floor, and the knee had said so, as a knee says a thing, and the clerk, who was careful, had seen a hand who did not stand like a hand, and had let his pen stop.

It was a half-breath. It was the place where the form had no column and a careful man could ask.

And Vastin — who had stood in front of a thousand clerks from the far side of a thousand counters, and had never once in his life been on the near side of one with a pen stopped on him — did the thing a lifetime had built him to do, which was to end the pause with the fact that ended pauses.

"Vastin," he said. "Archmarshal, Compact enforcement. You'll find the name is — "

Seln was there.

Cael did not see him come from the lane's mouth. He was simply at Vastin's shoulder, between the man and the door, close, with a hand on the kit's case as if to steady it, and his voice was the voice for an inventory, low, and he said, over the man's sentence and through it, to the clerk, "He's on the count. Four hands, same as yesterday, entered by the practitioner and answered for by her, and the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was. The researcher's at your counter and she'll want to be told we're through," and the clerk — who had heard a word he did not know the weight of, in a lane, from a man with a stick, and had heard another man say, in the voice that explains things, only what the form asked, and was young, and careful, and had a woman at his counter who would not stop talking about strata — looked at Vastin for one more half-breath, and at Seln, and wrote *four*, and said, "Down. Out by the bell," and went in.

Nobody moved for a moment.

Then Seln took his hand off the kit's case, and looked at Vastin, and did not say anything, and the not-saying was the worst thing Cael had ever seen Seln do to a man, because Seln said a thing once and this was the once and he was not spending it.

Vastin stood in the lane.

He had heard himself. That was the thing Cael catalogued, because it was the thing that cost: the man had heard the sentence come out of his own mouth — a name, a rank, the Compact's own title, spoken aloud in a lane behind a Compact registry in a Compact city on the Line by a man who had resigned from it, to a clerk, to smooth a door — and had heard it land. Not as authority. The clerk had not known the name. The rank was nothing at this level; the level had no column for it, and a rank that the form has no column for is a noise. And the third thing, the thing he had heard last and understood first: that a former Archmarshal invoking the Compact inside a Compact city, in front of a clerk who kept a book, with an unranked boy at his shoulder who had a piece of paper in an office in the west that said what he was, had just come within one careful man's question of closing every door on the hill, and had been stopped from it by a defector's tradecraft and a sentence that told the clerk nothing but what the form could hold.

Seln had not lied. Cael entered that, because it was the kind of thing a ledger should carry: a man who could have said anything in that lane — a carter's man, a factor from up west, any story a clerk would have taken — had said only what was true and let the form do the rest, and the rest had been enough, and the not-lying had cost him a longer sentence and a longer half-breath of the clerk's eye, and he had paid both without a flicker.

He stood with it. He stood in the lane with the kit's case on his shoulder and the stick in his hand and his face doing the thing a face does when a man has just found out that the reflex he is proudest of — the one that had ended every pause in his life, the fact that ended pauses — is not a tool any more but a blade with no handle, and that the only way he could help the four people in the lane with him was to be no one, and that he did not know how to be no one, and had not known that he did not know until a half-breath ago.

"I'm sorry," he said. To the lane. In the ledger voice, which had never in Cael's hearing said that sentence before.

"Don't be sorry," Seln said. "Be nobody. It's the only thing down here that's worth anything, and you can't buy it and you can't be given it. You have to stop being the other thing." He went back to the lane's mouth. "Down. She's waiting."

---

They went down. Cael carried the box. It was a long stair and the box was the middle one and he had carried it up and down twice now and knew its weight to the hair, and he carried it behind Brom and ahead of Lira with the satchel under it and the man with the stick behind Lira, going down slowly, on the knee, in silence, and at the iron door with the mark on it at the height of a man's eye he set the box down to open the door, because the hand nearest the door opened it, and that was him.

He looked at the man behind him while he did it.

Vastin was on the landing, with the case, looking at the iron. He was not looking at the mark. He was looking at the door as a thing that opened on a place where the name he had just spoken was worth nothing, not because the place had decided that, but because the place was older than the name, and he was, Cael thought, working out how a man walks into a place like that, and finding that he had never once in his life had to.

Cael had. He had walked into every room of his life as the thing the room could not use, and had learned, at fourteen, in a room with a door that locked from the outside, that being nothing to a room was a thing you could stand in if you stood in it on purpose. He had been no one for five years. He had been no one on purpose for nine months. He was, at that moment, on a landing under a city, the nearest thing on the continent to an expert in the exact thing a fifty-three-year-old man was standing behind him failing to do, and he did not say so, because a man who has learned a thing at a price does not hand it to the man who set the price for free, and because it was not his to hand; the man would learn it or he would not, and the learning was the man's.

He filed it. He filed the fellow-feeling, which was the strangest thing he had felt in a year that had had a good deal of strange in it — that he and the Compact's own held line had a thing in common now, on a landing, and the thing was a door that neither of their names would open — and he pushed the iron, and it went in on its pins without a sound, and the lamp went ahead of him into the dark, and he picked up the box.

---

*Two hundred and eighty-fifth day. Under Treswick. The Deepstacks — the old quarry under the registry, shelved, unread. Second day down. Four hands; Seln above. A hundred and thirty-six in hand; the inn paid to the week.*

*Karis has been looking for this room her whole life. Cleon's been sitting in it for thirty years waiting for her. Vastin tried to open a door with his rank and nearly closed all of them — an Archmarshal's worth nothing here, which he's taking about as well as I took [SHATTERED]. Down here the stone's older than the Compact, so the Compact doesn't reach. First place inside walls I've ever stood where nobody can file me, and it's a hole under a city, and it's because everything down here is older than the machine that files. Karis says give her a week. I've got a box to carry and a promise to keep about a station upstairs. I can wait.*
# Chapter 11 — The Marks

By the third day down he had learned the lamp.

That was the whole of his trade in the chamber, and he learned it as he had learned to hold a cord's far end for a woman who was pacing: where the light had to be for the reader, which was never where the reader was looking but a hand to the left of it, so that the hand did not shadow the line; when to move it, which was before she reached for it and not after; how to hold a sheet flat on the table's edge with two fingers at its corners so that a man could read the whole of a carving-copy without the paper's curl putting a false line across a true one. Labour. He was good at it by the second hour of the second day and very good at it by the third, and Cleon had stopped saying "Lamp" and had started simply turning his head a finger's width, and the light was there, and Cael found that he did not mind being a thing a man turned his head for, and wrote that down at the bell, because it was true and he had not expected it.

The two hundred and eighty-sixth day. The chamber under the registry, the lamp that was always lit and the one he held, Cleon in the cushioned chair and Karis in the one that had none, and on the table between them the Ring's eleven and the Stair's twenty-nine and the Hall's twelve, and beside the Hall's, on its own square of cloth, the thing Cleon had kept on the shelf behind him for ten years.

He catalogued it because Cleon had let him hold it, once, to move it into the light, and a thing you have held is a thing you owe a description. A board. Not a leaf; a flat piece of a wood he did not know, the length of a forearm and a hand wide, gone the colour of old iron, with a face to it, and on the face, cut in with something finer than a chisel, lines. Not letters. He knew the Compact's letters and had seen four scripts that were not the Compact's on the shelves in three days, and these were none of the five, so far as he could tell: lines that went across the board's face in runs, some straight and some bent at the ends, with the bends the same at the ends of some runs and different at the ends of others, so that all the face looked, if you held it far enough from your eye, like a wall Karis had copied. He had thought that on the first day and had not said it. On the third day he did not need to, because that was the day Cleon put the board beside the Hall's fourth sheet and said, "Now."

---

"You'll want to watch this," Cleon said, "because it's the only thing I do that's worth watching, and I've never done it in front of anyone."

He said it to the sheet. He had his right hand flat on the board and his left flat on the copy, a finger's width apart on the table, and his eyes were not on either; they were on the place between them, unfocused, like a man listening to two things at once and trying to hear whether they are the same sound.

"A carving's a made thing," he said. "Somebody cut it. Somebody decided where every line went, and every line's there for a reason, and the reasons aren't equal. Some lines carry. Some lines are carried. And some — " his finger moved on the board, not touching, above a short run at the edge — "some are nothing. Ornament. A man's hand doing what a hand does at the end of a row. I see which is which. That's all the Path. I put my attention on a made thing and I see the bones of it — which mark's holding the others up, which mark would bring the rest down if you cut it out, which mark you could cut out and nothing would notice." He moved his hand to the sheet. "Here. This one." Above a mark on the Hall's fourth copy, one of the forty-one kinds, a bent run with a hook at the end. "That's a bearer. Cut it and this whole row's nothing. And this — " a shorter run beside it — "is carried. It rests on the first. And this — " the smallest, at the row's end — "is a man finishing a line with his hand because the stone had room. Ornament. Nothing." He looked at Karis. "Do you see it?"

"No," Karis said. "I see forty-one kinds of mark in three strata. I don't see which ones hold."

"No. You wouldn't. It's not in the strata; it's in the make." He put his hand back on the board. "Now the board. Same thing. This run bears. These three rest on it. This at the end is a hand finishing a row." He was quiet a moment. "Same bones. The same three kinds in the same order, bearer and carried and ornament, in the same places in the row. I've read this board for ten years and I've known it had a structure and I've never once had a second thing with the same structure to put beside it, and now I have, and it's a floor."

Cael held the lamp a hand to the left of the board and did not move it, with Brom and Lira at the rock wall behind him and Vastin on the knee beside the case, all three of them as still as labour is still when it has nothing to carry, and watched a man read a made thing's skeleton, and understood, at arm's length, at the level of the hands, what the instrument was.

It was not the reading. Oryn's hands went into a thing and followed what flowed. This did not go in. It stood outside a made thing and saw how it had been put together — saw load — as the Iron read, on a floor, had told him where a thing the weight of a cart was pushing. But the Iron read felt weight. This saw *design*: which of a maker's choices were the ones the thing stood on. He had wanted, against a post across the river, an instrument for what did not move. This was an instrument for what had been *built*, and it was standing three feet from him, in a man's hands, being used on a wall.

"There's a second thing," Cleon said, "and it's the thing that matters, and I'll say it once and I'll say it plain, so you'll not go away thinking the Path does more than it does."

He sat back. He took both hands off the table.

"A made thing's either a record or an instruction. It describes, or it commands. A ledger describes: this much, on this day, to this man. A recipe commands: do this, then this. They're built differently — the bones are different — and I can see which is which as you can see whether a wall's holding a roof or just standing there. These" — the sheets — "command. All fifty-two. Every run on every floor is built as an instruction's built, with the bearers where an instruction puts them. They're not the marks somebody cuts to say *we were here*, and they're not the marks somebody cuts to make a floor handsome. They're orders." He looked at the board. "This is an order too. Same make."

Karis had not moved. Her pencil was on the notebook and had not touched it.

"And here's the limit, and I want it in your book in my words so nobody can say I claimed past it." He said it to her, and he said it slowly. "I can tell you these are orders. I can't tell you what they order. For that you need the language, and the language is in the text, not the stone. That's her work, not mine."

He looked at Karis when he said *her*, and she wrote it down, in his words, and Cael watched her write it, and saw her underline *not the stone*, once, because that was where the limit fell and she wanted the line under it.

"Structure," Cleon said. "Not meaning. It works on a made thing — a carving, a wall, a document, a thing with a maker's choices in it. It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree, because nobody chose where the branches went. It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else. It's no use to anyone in a fight, which is why I'm Bronze at sixty and have never once minded. And it can't read. I want that understood. I've had this board ten years and I can tell you every bone in it and I cannot tell you one word it says, because it doesn't say words to me. It says *load*. The words are in the text, in a hand I can half read, and the text's your work, and I've been waiting thirty years for someone to walk in and do it."

Karis asked him one thing, then, in the voice for a finding. "The three floors. Same make, all three?"

"Same make. Same bearers in the same places. The Hall's rows are longer and the Stair's are cut deeper, but the bones are the bones. Somebody cut three floors two days' walk apart in the same hand, or in three hands taught the same thing, and the thing they were taught is the thing on this board." He looked at the board. "I'll not say more than that. I've said more than I've said in thirty years."

"Noted," Karis said, and wrote it, and underlined nothing, because there was nothing in it yet to underline.

Cael held the lamp still.

*It doesn't work on a man.* He had it. He had wanted, without letting himself want it, to ask the question he had never let anyone hold — to put the one instrument on the continent that read built things onto the thing in the middle of him that did not flow and see what it said — and the man had just answered it without being asked, at a table, as a limit stated plainly so that nobody could claim past it. *Nobody chose where the branches went.* A man was not a made thing. The instrument did not go on him.

He noted that he had wanted it to. He noted that the wanting had been there since a post by a river, and that he had not said it, and that he was not going to, and that a thing which read only what had been built could not be turned on a person, and that he had been sorry, and that the sorriness was a thing he did not have a column for and was going to have to carry down a stair and up again with a box. He did not say any of it. He moved the lamp a hand to the left when Cleon turned his head, and the light was there.

---

Brom's offer came at the bell.

Cael was in the yard for it, because the bell had sent the hands up and out of the cellar in the order they had gone down, and Brom had been first down and was first up, and Cael, with the middle box, came through the inn's yard gate a dozen strides behind him and stopped, because there was a cart on its side in the yard and a man of Brom's size standing at it with a box set down carefully at his feet.

A broker's cart. It had come through the yard's gate too fast, by the evidence — a wheel in the drain, the bed over, the load shifted against the rails and held by the ropes — and the carter was at the beast's head with the traces fouled, and the broker's factor, a man in a coat that had a colour, stood at the cart's tail with a ledger under his arm and the look of a man pricing a loss.

Brom set his shoulder to the cart's bed.

He did it without a Path, because a healer had said not a door, not a box, not a man, and Brom kept a price; with the good shoulder and his back and his legs, and the foot with the tooth-hole in it taking the brace on the stones, and the bed came up, and hung, and the good shoulder said something Cael saw in the set of it and Brom did not, and the cart went back onto its wheels with the load on and stood there. Brom stepped back from it and put a hand on the box at his feet as if the box were the thing that wanted seeing to.

The factor watched him do it. He watched him set the box down first and touch the cart second, and Cael saw him price it — the size of the man; the care; the Copper's paper that a man in that trade could see on a stranger as a smith sees a shoe — and the factor did not wait, because a broker who sees a thing worth having does not.

"You've paper," he said. It was not a question.

"Copper," Brom said. "Circuit-rated."

"I've a yard here and a second on the second tier, and I've no one in either who could have done that without a Path, and two who couldn't have done it with one." He named the wage. It was more than the five had earned in the winter, and he said it as a man says a thing he knows is enough. "Guard's post. A pass — not the district's nothing; a working pass to the second tier with my name on it, and a bed inside the second wall. You'd be ranked, here. You'd be what you are."

Brom listened to all of it. Cael, at the gate with the middle box, watched him listen — the shoulder still, the face he used for a wall somebody else was pricing — and knew from the face that Brom was pricing it honestly, because Brom priced everything honestly, and that it was a good price. It was the life. It was the one his family had wanted from him, at fourteen, with a Gold in their eye and a Copper in his hand; the one he had walked away from into a circuit and then into a road and then across a Line; the one that had been waiting, it turned out, in a broker's yard on the Line for a man big enough to put a cart back on its wheels. It was clean. Nobody at Lowmarch's plank had ever offered him anything clean.

Brom picked up the box while the factor was still talking. He put it on the shoulder that was not the good one, because the good one had just paid for a cart and he knew it, and he would carry the bound one wrong for a day and say nothing, and Cael entered the debt where debts went.

"I'll think on it," Brom said, which was courtesy, because Brom did not lie.

Then he went back across the yard with the box on the wrong shoulder toward the inn's door and the morning and the stair and the hole under the city where a woman was reading a wall, and the factor watched him go with the face of a man who has priced a thing correctly and been declined by it, and did not understand, and did not need to.

That was the whole statement. Cael wrote it as one, at the bell: *Brom was offered the life. He picked up the box.*

He asked Cael one thing about it, later, on the stair, going down, with the boxes.

"You think Karis is right? That it's all built on something?"

"I think we're about to find out."

Brom shifted the box. "Good. I'd hate to have turned down a real job for a wrong hunch."

He was joking. Cael heard the joke. He also heard the other thing, under it, which was a man who had put a cart back on its wheels with his back because a healer had said not to use his shoulder, and had been offered a bed inside a wall for it, and had picked up a box, and wanted, very slightly, to be told that the box was going somewhere. Cael did not tell him. He did not know. He said, "So would I," which was true, and they went down.

---

Seln walked the tiers that night.

Cael did not see him go and did not see him come back, and knew both, because a man who has slept in the same room as Seln for a year knows the shape of the room with Seln in it and the shape without, and woke at the second shape, some hour after the city's bell had gone for the last time, and lay in the dark and did not get up, because the thing Seln was doing was Seln's.

He knew what it was. He had known since the ferry. The cache was on Seln's back — had been on Seln's back for four years, across a hearing and a Line and a rim with four crossbows in the scrub, carried and never opened and targeted once and kept — and it had walked, four days ago, into the first governed city Seln had stood in since he had walked out of one with it. And up the hill, on the crown, past three gates and a square, there was a registry, with a book in it and a counter and a careful young man with a pen, and behind the registry, somewhere in the west, the office the registry answered to, which would have paid for what was in the cache in a currency Seln had stopped taking, in a room he had stopped standing in, on a day he had stopped counting from.

He had not decided anything. That was the thing Cael understood in the dark, without a word from anyone: that a man who carries a thing for four years without opening it is not a man who has decided to keep it. He is a man who has decided not to decide, every day, and has found that the not-deciding is a thing you can carry, and has carried it, and has walked it now into the one place on the road where the decision had a counter to be made at. Seln had gone up the hill to look at the counter. Not to use it. To look. As he had looked at a landing door for a winter, and at four crossbows in scrub, and at a sheet with a bar of ink on it: to know where a thing was, exactly, so that he could keep on not going to it on purpose instead of by accident.

How he went up three tiers at night with no pass was not a thing Cael asked, then or after. He had a guess. The stairs cut in the bluff had rails, and the rails ended where the walls began, and the walls were old, and Seln had told a room once that a city's walls were built by people who wanted to keep things out and not by people who had ever tried to get in. He had a guess and he kept it, because a guess about Seln was a thing you kept.

Seln came back before the light. Cael heard the room change shape and did not move, and Seln crossed to his blanket and lay down, and was still, and then said, to the dark, in the voice for an inventory, very low, so that only the one person in the room who was awake would have it:

"I looked at it."

That was all. Cael did not answer. He lay in the dark and had, from a man who said a thing once, the shape of the thing, which was as much as Seln ever gave, and the grit on a boot, which Cael had heard on the floor and not seen, said a roof: that Seln had gone up in the night — not to the crown; Seln did not stand beside a thing that read bodies; to somewhere under it, a roof in the district or the foot of a stair, with the cache on his back and the station's pale light above him — and had looked up at the door his old trade would have walked through, and had decided nothing, and had come back down, seen by nobody, and had let one person know that he had decided nothing, which was the nearest thing to trust that Seln had ever handed anyone, and was, Cael thought, a great deal.

He wrote it down in the morning in six words, because six was what it was. *Seln looked at it. Decided nothing.*

Lira, who had also been awake — she was always awake when the room changed shape, and had been since a ford — said nothing about it at all, then or after, which was the other half of the thing: a crew that keeps a man's not-deciding for him without once asking him to decide.

---

Vastin, that week, was learning to be nobody, and was bad at it.

Cael catalogued the badness because it was honest and because he had never once seen a man try. The man carried the kit's case down the stair each morning on the knee and set it down against the rock and stood beside it, and did not draft anything, and did not offer anything, and did not say his name to anyone, and the not-doing sat on him like a coat cut for a bigger man. He had stood at the head of things for forty years, and standing at the foot of them was a thing his body did not know the shape of: he stood too straight for a hand; he watched the readers like an officer at a briefing and not like labour watching a woman work; and when Cleon turned his head for the lamp, Vastin's hand moved before Cael's did, every time, and stopped, because the lamp was Cael's, and the stopping was the whole of his week.

On the fourth day he said, to Cael, at the rock wall, very quietly, "How long did it take you."

"To be nobody?"

"Yes."

"I was fourteen. I'm nineteen." Cael moved the lamp. "I'm not there yet either."

Vastin took that. He did not draft anything. He stood at the foot of a thing, badly, and kept standing.

---

Late on the fourth day, with the bell an hour off and the lamp low, Cleon put the Hall's fourth sheet beside the board for the last time that day and sat looking at the two of them for a long while without reading either, and then said, to Karis, in a voice Cael had not heard from him — not the voice for a limit; the other one, the one a man keeps for a thing he has carried alone:

"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." He tapped the board. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." He sat back. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." A beat. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."
# Chapter 12 — No Authority

The alley had steps in it, and that was all the geography, and he had it before the first man moved because he had walked the alley eleven times with a box on his shoulder and a cataloguer walks a thing eleven times so that he has it on the twelfth.

The first tier's stair came down off the bluff to a landing at the first wall's foot, and from the landing the route to the ring road was not the wide street the carts used but a cut between two rows of houses that Seln had found on the second day and used since, because a working party that went home by the carts' street was a working party that could be counted going home. The cut was stepped — twenty-two steps, in five flights, each flight a stride and a half wide and walled on both sides by the backs of houses with no doors in them, three men high, sheer — and it fell from the landing to the inn's yard gate in a straight line that a man at the top could see the bottom of, and a man at the bottom could see the top of, and nobody in between could see anything but wall. A choke. He had thought that on the second day, and had said it to Seln, and Seln had said, "Yes. That's why. Nobody holds a choke they can't get out of, so nobody holds this one," and had been right for eight days.

On the two hundred and ninetieth day, at dusk, with the boxes, coming down the third flight, he saw that somebody had decided to hold it after all.

Four at the top. He counted them as he had counted hounds and hoods — without seeing one clearly — as they came out of the landing's shadow onto the first flight above and behind the party: four figures, not carters, not in a colour, with their hoods up in a city where nobody wore a hood at dusk in spring, and one of them with a thing at his shoulder that was a crossbow, in a city, which was a thing nobody carried in a city unless they had brought it for a purpose. And two at the bottom, coming through the inn's yard gate from the ring road, which they had gone round by, with staves, into the fifth flight, so that the cut had a lid on it and a floor, and the party — Karis, and four hands with four boxes, and the cache on a man who was not there — was in the middle three flights, on steps, with wall on either side, and no room to do anything a Path did except be loud.

He catalogued it because a fight is drawn before it is fought. Six of them. A crossbow above. Staves below. Twenty-two steps, sheer walls, dusk. Karis on the third flight with the flat. Brom above her with the first box on the good shoulder and the bound one carried. Lira on the second flight with the third box on two arms. Vastin on the fourth flight, below them all, with the kit's case and the stick, on the knee, nearest the two coming up. And Cael on the third flight beside Karis with the middle box and the satchel under it, ten things in him and one of them a burst that would have been the loudest thing in the cut and would have brought a bolt down it from the top in the beat after, which was, he understood, what the crossbow was for: they had come for practitioners. They had come for a crew that walked out of quiet ground declaring, and they had built the trap for the declaration, and the trap was a stone cut with a bolt at the top of it.

Seln was not anywhere. Good.

"Boxes down," Cael said. Not loud. "Karis, against the wall. Nobody declares."

The boxes went down. That was the sound of it, four boxes on stone steps, and the four at the top heard it and came down the first flight fast, because a party that put its boxes down was a party that was going to fight, and a crossbow that has been brought to end a declaration does not wait for it.

Vastin turned round.

---

He rooted the choke above them.

Cael had seen it done once, on a road, across a cut with ice in it, and had learned then what a wall was and what it cost, and had thought, since, that he knew all of it. He had not. On a road a wall was a plane across a line with nothing on either side of it but scree, and a pack had gone round it on the loose slope, slow, and the man holding it had been the loudest thing in a country with nothing behind him but a man with a rock. This was a cut. This was a stride and a half of stone between two walls three men high with no door in either, and Vastin turned on the fourth flight — turned uphill, with his back to the two coming up from the yard gate and his face to the four coming down from the landing, which was the wrong way round for a man defending himself and the only way round for a man defending a party — and went past Lira and Brom and Karis and Cael on the third flight, on the knee, with the stick, faster than a man with that knee had any business going up steps, and stopped on the second flight below the four, and set his feet, and the plane went up.

Cael went up after him. Two steps, from the third flight to the second's top, with the box left where it lay and the knife out, because the plane's foot was where the reason for the plane should stand, and he stood there, two feet below it, and did not go further, because further was the plane.

Across the cut. Wall to wall, because the cut's walls were a stride and a half apart and a plane knows what it was told, and the walls were sheer and the plane went from one to the other without a hair of a gap, and from the second flight's top step to somewhere above a man's head, and it was, Cael saw — from two feet below it, looking up — the first thing he had ever watched Iron Wall do that the ground did not argue with. On a road the ground had said *go round*. Here the ground said *there is no round*. Three of the four at the top came down the first flight at a run and hit the plane as eight hounds had hit it, and did not go over each other, because they were men and men do not; they went into a thing that was not there and stopped as if it were the cut's own stone, and the crossbow, which had been coming up to a shoulder, went off into the plane a hand from the man's face, and the bolt did what the ice's bolts had done on a floor, which was nothing, and fell.

And the man holding it could not move.

He knew that already. He had known it on a road and had made a plan around it and had watched the plan need every one of them. But he saw the other half now, in a cut, which the road had not shown him: that in a corridor with sheer walls, a wall that cannot move does not need to. There was no flank. There was no scree for a hound to take slow. There was the plane, and the four above it, and the four could go through it or not, and they could not, and every other way to the party was round by the ring road and up through the yard gate, which was a hundred strides and a minute, and a minute, in a fight, was the whole of a fight.

"Hold it," Cael said, which was not an order, because the man was going to hold it whatever anyone said; it was a count, so that the man would know somebody was counting.

"I'm holding it."

The two from the yard gate came up the fifth flight.

---

That was the second thing, and it was the thing a road had taught the crew and the team had not learned, and it was all the difference.

They came up with staves, two of them, hooded, fast, into the fourth flight where Vastin had been and was not, and they came up ready for what they had been told to be ready for, which was a declaration: a burst, a hardening, a lattice, a thing that would tell them where to swing and how hard. They had built a trap for a crew that walked out of quiet ground declaring. They had a crossbow at the top for the loud one and two staves at the bottom for the rest — for the big one who had righted a cart in a yard where anyone could watch, and for the boy from the report, both of them on the wall's protected side — and the whole of their plan was that a practitioner, cornered, declares, and a declaration is a place to hit.

Nobody declared.

Brom came down off the third flight to meet the first stave, and did not go hard, because a healer had said not a door, not a box, not a man, and because he had learned at a ford that Iron Skin was a shout; he came down as a very large man with a rock in his good hand, which he had picked up off the fourth step where the cut's makers had left a loose one a long time ago, and put his shoulder into the stave's man as a man puts a shoulder into a cart that is on its side, which was a thing he had done once this week already, and the stave's man went into the wall, and the stave went down the steps, and Brom stood on it. He did not hit the man again. He had not needed to. He stood on the stave with his bound shoulder carried still and forward and his good hand with the rock in it and looked at the second man coming up, and the second man, who had been told to expect a practitioner, looked at a big man with a rock standing on his friend's stave, and did not know what to hit.

Lira told him.

She had no room. That was the cut's whole price for her: a woman who was fast had a stride and a half of stone and nowhere to run in it, and she had known it since the second day and had walked the cut anyway, and now she did the thing a fast woman does when there is no room, which is to use the one direction the walls do not close: down. She came off the second flight's edge onto the man's shoulders from above, two-armed, with the knife, and the knife did not go into him — Cael saw that, and saw her choose it — it went into the stave's shaft where his hands were, and the man let go of a thing that had a knife in it, and Lira came down off him onto the steps on her own foot and put the flat of the knife across the back of his knee, and he sat down, on the fourth flight, hard, and did not get up, because a man who has just been landed on from a height does not.

Two staves on the steps. Two men on the steps. Nobody hit twice. Nobody declared.

Cael had not moved. That was his whole part in the second thing, and he catalogued it because it was the thing he had learned on a road and was doing on purpose: he had stayed at the plane's foot, on the second flight's top step, beside the man who could not move, with the knife out and the satchel under his arm and his back to Karis against the wall, and had been the reason. The four above the plane could see him. He was the boy — the one the completion report had named, the one who had walked out of the Quiet declaring — and he was six feet from their crossbow with nothing but a plane between, standing still, not declaring, being the thing they had come for and giving them nothing to hit, and every one of the four wanted through that plane worse than they wanted anything, because he was on the other side of it, and the wanting was the cost of the plane, and he was the cost.

*Someone else has to be the reason.* He had the sentence now. He had had it since a road. He was it.

---

Seln took the top of the cut from the roof.

Cael did not see it. He saw the result, which was the thing you saw with Seln, and drew the rest afterward, because a fight is drawn after as well as before.

Three of the four above the plane had come down onto the first flight. The fourth had not. He was on the landing at the cut's head, in the shadow where the stair came down, standing where a man stands who is not going to fight and is going to tell the others when to — and Cael had counted six and had had six, four and two, and had been right about the number and wrong about what one of them was for, because the one you do not count until he speaks was the one he had counted as a fighter. He spoke. He said one word up the cut, from the landing, to the crossbow, a word Cael did not catch and did not need to, because a word said from the back of a fight to the front of it is a signal, and a signal is a coordination, and a coordination is a thing you can remove.

Seln removed it.

He was on the roof of the house on the cut's east side — he had been there, Cael worked out later, since the party had come down the first flight, because Seln went home by a roof when a cut had two ends and he could only watch one — and the landing was under the roof's edge, and the sixth man was on the landing, and a man on a landing in a city at dusk does not look up, because nobody in a city looks up. A cord. That was all it was. A weighted cord, from above, round the sixth man's arm and the rail he stood beside, so that the arm he signalled with was tied to the stair, and then Seln was not on the roof any more and the sixth man was pulling at his own arm in the shadow and saying nothing, because a man tied to a rail does not say the next word; he works at the cord. Nobody died. Nobody was cut. The signal stopped.

And the three at the plane, who had been waiting for the word that told them when to go round by the ring road and take the party from below with the two who were now sitting on the steps, did not get the word, and stood at a plane with their hoods up and a crossbow they had spent and a leader who had gone quiet, and were, for a count of three, six people who did not know what to do.

That was the third thing. The cache was on the roof. It had been on the roof the whole time, on a man's back, unopened, and the four had come for it, and it had been above them, and they had never once looked up.

---

The fourth thing was that they left.

He catalogued it as the ford had taught him to catalogue a pack that has lost its first mover: not a rout; a *decision*, taken all at once by six people who had built a plan for a declaration and had got a wall, a rock, a knife in a stave, a cord round an arm, and a boy standing still, and had understood, in the time it takes a man to draw one, that the plan was not going to happen and that the next plan would be made somewhere else. The three at the plane went back up the first flight into the landing's shadow, and one of them cut the fourth man's cord with a knife, and the four of them were four shapes on a landing, and then they were not, because a landing in a city has three ways off it and they took all three. The two on the steps got up — the one with the knee, slowly — and went down past Vastin's plane on the wrong side of it, which was the party's side, because the party let them, because Brom stood on the stave and Lira stood on the step and neither of them touched a man who was walking away, and the two went through the inn's yard gate into the ring road and were gone into a district that had a thousand people in it and no faces.

None taken. No faces. Nobody dead. Two staves and a spent bolt on the steps, and a weighted cord on a rail, and six hoods that had gone into a city like water into ground.

The plane came off.

Vastin went down on the second flight as he had gone down on a road, both knees and then a hand, except that it was worse — a Silver-tier plane held across a cut for a minute and some is not a plane held across a road for the seconds a charge takes — and the knee that had gone under him on a road eleven days ago went under him again on a step, and he sat on the step with his back to the cut's wall and his face the colour of the plane and did not say anything, and could not have, and Cael, who was two feet below him with a knife in his hand, put the knife away and went up the two feet and sat down on the step beside him, because the man could not move and somebody should be where he could not move to.

---

He catalogued the costs on the step, because the costs were the ledger and there was no healer to catalogue them for him.

Karis: a cut, on the back of the left hand, shallow, from the stone — she had gone down against the wall when the boxes went down and had put her hand on a step's edge and the edge had been a step's edge — bleeding as a shallow cut on a hand bleeds, which is more than it should. Brom bound it. He did it with Karis's thread and a strip off his own shirt, on the steps, with his good hand and his teeth, badly, like a man who has watched a healer do it a hundred times and has never once been the hands, and Karis held the hand out and looked at the flat under her other arm and said, "The sheets are dry," which was her whole report on the cut. No healer inside the gate would put hands on an unranked woman's hand: Oryn had priced a Copper's shoulder at a fire, and a hand with no column at all was the same arithmetic, further down, and the cost of it was a strip of shirt and Brom's teeth, and Cael wrote the cost down as she had priced it, because she had priced it exactly.

Brom: the shoulder, carried through all of it, still and forward, not used, and Cael saw what the not-using had cost — a man who had put his shoulder into a stave's man with the wrong shoulder, the good one, from the wrong side, because the right one was bound, and had done it badly and had done it anyway — and Brom sat on the stave with the rock still in his hand and said, "Cart was heavier," and did not say the other thing.

Lira: nothing. Two arms, twenty-five days. She was standing on the fourth flight with the knife wiped, opening the left hand and closing it, and she caught him looking, and did not stop this time.

Seln: back, by the yard gate, with the cache on his back and nothing in his hands and the cord left on the rail because a cord on a rail was a thing that could be found and a man could not, and he came up the steps and looked at the two staves and the bolt and said, "They'll go to ground and think. They know where we sleep now. They knew before, but now they know we know." He looked at the plane's place, where the air had stopped being stone. "They came for a declaration. They got a wall and a rock. They don't know what to make of that, and they'll want to."

Cael entered the limit under it, as a finding and not an aside: the six now held the inn, the hour and the route, and what they had seen was a plane and a rock in an alley; what they had *not* seen was what a Path did inside quiet ground, because nobody had, and an alley was not a floor, and a team that built for a declaration in a cut would build for something else at a line, and he did not know what.

And Vastin.

Cael did not have a catalogue for it, and had not had one on a road either, and had one now, because he had sat beside it while the man's breath came back.

He had stood two feet from it. That was the fact. He had stood on the second flight's last step, at the plane's foot, for all of it — a minute; he had counted it, because he counted things, and it had been a minute and some — with a crossbow above and his own body the reason the crossbow wanted through, and had watched, from two feet, a Silver-tier Iron Wall held across a corridor by a man who could not move and would not stop, and the plane had been *there*, the whole minute, at the edge of his eye, a place where the air had been told it was stone and had believed it, and the believing had been the man, and the man had been the plane's foot, and Cael had stood beside the foot and been the reason the plane was needed, for a minute, under real threat, with nothing in him declared.

And he had it.

He did not know when. Somewhere in the minute. Not at the start, when the four hit the plane; not at the end, when it came off; somewhere in the middle, in the long part where nothing happened and the plane held and the four stood at it and the crossbow was spent and he stood still, the thing had gone into him the way the ten had gone in — not taken; *learned*, at two feet, by a body that learned the shape of a thing by standing beside it long enough — and he had felt it go and had not looked at it, because he was being the reason and the reason does not look away. He looked now. On the step, beside a man who could not move, with the costs catalogued and the cut empty, he put his attention where the thing was, the way he had been taught to on a table, and it rendered.

*Iron-Wall-adjacent. Vastin. Partial. Iron family. Held. A plane, not a body: the ground told not to give. Distinct from the third — Iron-adjacent, the bed; this is not the bed hardened, this is a line drawn on it and kept. Engagement field: defensive; rooted. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Eleventh.*

He read it once and let it stand and did not use it. There was nothing to use it on; the cut was empty. He sat with it. The man beside him breathed.

"You held it," Cael said.

"I held it." Vastin's voice was the ledger's, with nothing left in the ledger. "There was nothing behind it. I have never once in my life held a line with nothing behind it. There's always been — " He stopped. "A field. A formation. The Compact. I held it because I decided to, and that was all that was behind it, and it held."

"It held."

"It nearly didn't. I could feel the place where it wouldn't have." He looked at his own hands, on his knees, on a step. "I have put that plane in front of a great many people in forty years. I have never once put it in front of anyone the Compact would have restricted." He did not look at Cael. "I know what you are, on the paper. I have read the paper more times than any man alive. I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered somewhere, since I'm not the one who keeps the ledger any more."

Cael did not say anything for a while. Then he said, "It's entered," and took the log out, on the step, and wrote it, and did not tell the man what else he had entered, because a fragment is a thing you own and a thing a man gave you is a thing you owe, and he did not yet have a word for the distance between those two, and was not going to make one up on a step.

---

*Two hundred and ninetieth day. Treswick, the cut between the first-tier stair and the inn's yard. Six of them, faceless, hooded, a crossbow and staves; one of the six on the landing to say when. They came for a declaration. None taken. Nobody dead. The cache kept — it was on a roof. Karis, left hand, a cut, bound with thread and a shirt. Brom, the shoulder carried; the good one used wrong. Lira, nothing, two arms. Seln, nothing; a cord on a rail. Vastin, spent past a road; the knee again. Me, the burst not used, on purpose; nothing declared; a minute at a plane's foot, being the reason. A hundred and thirty-six in hand.*

*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. I've spent five years learning to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*

